{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel were in the wilderness.</b> It is known that the entire episode of the spies and the episode of the wood-gatherer, as well as Korah, all took place in the wilderness. What is the reason it mentions here 'and the children of Israel were in the wilderness'? It is possible to explain that it is to emphasize the enormity of the sin of the wood-gatherer, and therefore it was necessary to mention 'in the wilderness' and to say that in the wilderness where Israel were, and where the manna descended every day — which is among the well-known miracles indicating the renewal of the world — there this wood-gatherer was found to desecrate the Sabbath and to come against that renewal. Or one may say, 'and the children of Israel were in the wilderness' — because a decree had already been issued on account of the sin of the spies that they would remain there, as it is written above (Numbers 14:35): 'in this wilderness they shall be consumed and there they shall die' — so it was still necessary to say 'and the children of Israel were in the wilderness' and 'they found a man gathering wood,' meaning that it was fitting for the children of Israel to be in the wilderness even apart from the decree issued for the sin of the spies, because there were other transgressions among them, such as Sabbath-desecrators and those who treated the commandment of tzitzit lightly — and this is the juxtaposition of the passages. And moreover, all Israel are guarantors for one another, in accordance with what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Leviticus 26:37): 'וכשלו איש באחיו' — 'they shall stumble, a man because of his brother' — a man stumbles because of the sin of his brother." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain further: since it was said above, 'and the Amalekite and the Canaanite dwell in the valley,' meaning to lie in wait for you, 'tomorrow turn and journey toward the wilderness' — that they should go back by way of the Sea of Reeds — Scripture tells us here that they did as HaShem, may He be blessed, said and traveled backward into the wilderness by way of the Sea of Reeds, and immediately upon their arrival there in obedience to the command of HaShem, may He be exalted, the episode of the wood-gatherer occurred. And therefore it says 'and the children of Israel were,' to make known that they themselves caused this transgression, on account of their sin that required them to turn back — for a transgression drags in its wake another transgression." "<b>Or</b> it is possible to explain further that it is joined to the passage concerning idolatry that is adjacent to it. For the passage began with 'וכי תשגו' to explain the law of the community that worships idolatry unwittingly, and it concludes with the law of one who worships idolatry intentionally: 'והנפש אשר תעשה ביד רמה' — 'and the person who acts with a high hand' — 'הכרת תכרת עונה בה' — 'that person shall be cut off; his iniquity is upon him' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'עונה בה' means that his iniquity remains upon him, that is, he did not repent. And therefore it mentions 'and the children of Israel were,' for such was the conduct of the children of Israel in the wilderness when they made the calf — they worshipped idolatry there, at first unwittingly, as they said (Exodus 32:1): 'קום עשה לנו אלהים אשר ילכו לפנינו כי זה משה האיש' — 'arise, make us elohim that shall go before us, for this Moses, the man' — but in the end they acted intentionally, as it is written (Exodus 32:8): 'וישתחוו ויזבחו לו' — 'and they bowed down and sacrificed to it' — and even so they were forgiven, as it says (Numbers 14:20): 'סלחתי כדברך' — 'I have pardoned according to your word.'" "<b>It is also</b> possible to say that the mention of the wilderness in the sin of the wood-gatherer further alludes to anyone who travels in the wilderness on a distant road and does not know which day it is — he is still obligated to keep the Sabbath and is not permitted to exempt himself on that account. How should he act? He counts six days and rests on the seventh, as our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned. And the reason for this, by way of truth, is that each and every one of the seven is a Sabbath, for during the six days of Creation all seven acted and rested, and when the Sabbath came and performed its action of rest and delight, then all of them rested from their action, and then there was rest for them all, for themselves as well. And even though Israel in the wilderness kept track of the days and the years by calculation and could not err, Scripture alludes to you regarding anyone who travels on the road and does not know on which day the Sabbath falls — that he is liable to death if he does not keep the Sabbath, even in the wilderness." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And the children of Israel were in the wilderness' — he was Zelophehad son of Hepher. It is written here 'in the wilderness,' and it is written there (Numbers 27:3) 'our father died in the wilderness.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:55.451141914Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}